      "explanation": "Feedback regulation is seen in the increased heart rate in response to a decrease in blood\npressure. This is because, in feedback regulation, the homeostatic response (increased heart rate)\noccurs after the change (decrease in blood pressure) has occurred.\nOn the other hand, in feedforward regulation, the homeostatic responses take place before the\nchange has occurred. This is done in anticipation of the change to minimize fluctuations.\nExamples of feedforward regulation include:\n¢ The increased heart rate that occurs in an athlete just before a sprint begins.\n¢ The smell of food triggers the secretion of salivary and gastric juices.\n¢ An increase in insulin before glucose absorption has raised blood glucose levels.\"\nak ae\n.: S| Ps,"
